     "construction": {
      "name": "alternating_whole_triangle_strip_return",
      "proof": "ALTERNATING WHOLE-TRIANGLE STRIP RETURN\n\nThe ordinary bounding cell loses half its area for an equilateral triangle.\nRecover that area by a physical marked return before attempting a metric\nrealization. Put a=1/sqrt(3). The canonical unit-edge triangle has vertices\n(a,0),(-a/2,1/2),(-a/2,-1/2). For index t>=0, use its identity frame when\nt is even and its half-turn about z when t is odd. Put its center at\n((t mod 2)*a/2,t/2,0). Consecutive triangles share a complete oblique edge;\ntheir interiors lie on opposite sides of that edge. Nonconsecutive\ntriangles have disjoint y interiors except possible shared boundary.\nExtruding each complete triangle through the same prism height L keeps\nthe same nonoverlap and all whole-owner identities.\n\nA strip of m such prisms lies in the exact rectangle\n[-a/2,a] by [-1/2,m/2], extruded through [-L/2,L/2]. Its dimensions are\nsqrt(3)/2, (m+1)/2, L. Its midpoint is (a/4,(m-1)/4,0).\nThe additional half-unit of height is the retained pair of end costs;\nit may not be discarded by an infinite-strip density argument.\n\nPut complete strips in disjoint rectangular grid cells using these exact\ndimensions. A grid A by B by C fits in the common cube of side\nmax(A*sqrt(3)/2,B*(m+1)/2,C*L), and contains A*B*C*m complete prisms.\nKeep an explicitly indexed subset of N whole owners. This is a complete\nfinite return, not a marginal planar capacity statement.\n\nThe source is the classical equilateral triangular tiling and Cartesian\ncomposition. PingYou Ltd supplies the finite strip/grid assemblies and\ntheir certificates, without claiming the classical tiling as new. The\nzero-gain ordinary-length branch and every tested finite strip length\nremain useful controls. Native witnesses round the prescribed centers,\napply one common tiny outward dilation, retain the exact two half-turn\nquaternions, and are checked on every actual host wall and owner pair.\n",
